  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nPatchy low clouds are developing over the coastal areas and far\nwestern valleys this morning with just a few high clouds\nelsewhere. Marine layer low clouds will continue to develop\nthrough the morning with the greatest coverage in San Diego\nCounty, then clear by late morning. \n\nSatellite water vapor imagery shows an upper level trough just off \nthe West Coast and a weak short wave moving through the Bay Area.\nThis trough will bring us some long-awaited cooling as high\ntemperatures return to near normal today. Highs will be in the\nlow to mid 80s in the coastal areas, upper 80s to mid 90s for the\nwestern valleys, mid to upper 90s in the inland valleys, 70s and\n80s in the mountains, mid 90s in the high desert, and around\n100-108 in the low desert. No signs of dew points dropping yet\nfor the coastal areas and lower elevation valleys though, so \ndespite some relief from the heat it will remain humid. \n\nThe upper level trough remains in place for much of the week with\nseveral short waves moving through the mean flow. This will\nmaintain high temperatures near to a few degrees below normal\nthrough Friday. The marine layer rebuilds through this period for\nmore widespread night and morning low clouds for the coastal areas\nand western valleys. The GEFS and Canadian ensembles have backed\noff on the amount of moisture that gets pulled into So Cal from\nTropical Cyclone Karina on Tuesday and Wednesday. While we may\nstill get an increase in mid and high clouds from this system,\nprecipitation chances have lowered to around 5-15%. \n\nLate in the week, ensembles begin to diverge on the placement of\nthe upper trough near the West Coast and the upper high over the\nSouthern Rockies, with a trend towards a more eastward placement\nof each. This more eastward placement of the ridge and troughing\ncloser to the coast would limit the amount of warming late in the\nweek into next weekend. This would also affect the steering flow\nof a potential tropical system and any moisture and precipitation\nwe'd receive from it over the weekend into early next week. While\nthe National Hurricane Center has a 90% chance of this disturbance\ndeveloping into a tropical cyclone off the coast of Mexico in the\nnext 7 days, it is far too early to tell what, if any, impacts \nwe'll see locally, especially given the ensemble spread in the \nupper level pattern. Current forecast has at least a chance of \nprecipitation next Sunday.",
